{
  "source": "Bies 5e Table 6.2, PDF pages 367-369 (printed pp. 338-340)",
  "about": "The fifty-nine rows Table 6.2, \"Sabine absorption coefficients for some commonly used materials\", prints over three pages, in eight groups headed in italics: concert hall seats, walls, acoustic material, floors, ceilings, curtains, glass and other. Columns as printed: \"Material\" and, under \"Octave band centre frequency (Hz)\", 63, 125, 250, 500, 1000, 2000 and 4000; the 63 Hz column is filled on two rows only. Fifty-seven rows are coefficients and two are not: the page prints \"Audience, per person seated\" and \"standing\" with S alpha-bar (m2) beside the name, the absorption area of Equation (6.78) on printed page 337, and those two are held as areas per person rather than as coefficients. Four rows are headings with no numbers of their own (the concert hall with no seats, the smooth plaster, the fibreglass or rockwool blanket, the light velour), and their indented sub-rows are held with the heading as the name and the sub-row as the variant. No cell carries a footnote marker and the pages print no footnote. The only credit on the page is the \"(Beranek and Hidaka, 1998)\" printed inside the first row's name. What the book says of the whole table is on printed page 337 (PDF page 366): the values are \"selected from the literature\", a Sabine coefficient \"is dependent on the sample size, sample distribution and the properties of the room in which it is measured\", reverberation times calculated from them \"are approximate only\", and \"the approximate nature of the available data makes it desirable to either use manufacturer's data or take measurements (if possible)\". No mounting is printed for any row. Two cells of the 48 kg/m3, 50 mm blanket print one decimal, 0.3 and 0.8, where the rest of the table prints two.",
